Quiz title: PUBH 402 Final Exam — Draft Question Bank
Quiz description: Draft bank for the PUBH 402 final exam. Mix of baseline factual items, short-scenario causal reasoning items, and misclassification/boundary items. Items can be pulled into question groups for randomized administration.

% =====================================================================
% SECTION 1 — BASELINE FACTUAL ITEMS (Organization, Financing, Payment)
% These cover Course Learning Objectives 1 and 3 (describe organization
% and financing; describe approaches across levels of care). Recall floor.
% =====================================================================

Title: B01 Medicare Part A
Points: 1
1.  A 70-year-old enrolled in traditional Medicare is admitted to a hospital for a hip replacement. Which part of Medicare primarily pays for the inpatient hospital stay?
a)  Part B
*b) Part A
c)  Part C
d)  Part D

Title: B02 Medicaid financing structure
Points: 1
1.  Which statement best describes how Medicaid is financed in the United States?
a)  It is fully funded by the federal government, with states administering benefits.
*b) It is jointly financed by federal and state governments, with states administering the program within federal rules.
c)  It is financed primarily through enrollee premiums, like Medicare Part B.
d)  It is financed through a dedicated payroll tax, like Medicare Part A.

Title: B03 ACA Medicaid expansion
Points: 1
1.  Under the Affordable Care Act, states that expanded Medicaid extended eligibility primarily to which group?
a)  Children in households below 100% of the federal poverty level
*b) Adults under 65 with incomes up to 138% of the federal poverty level
c)  All uninsured adults regardless of income
d)  Adults aged 55–64 only

Title: B04 ESI dominant source
Points: 1
1.  Which is the largest single source of health insurance coverage for the non-elderly U.S. population?
a)  Medicare
b)  Medicaid
*c) Employer-sponsored insurance
d)  Direct-purchase individual market plans

Title: B05 EMTALA
Points: 1
1.  Which federal law requires hospital emergency departments that participate in Medicare to screen and stabilize anyone who presents with an emergency medical condition, regardless of ability to pay?
a)  HIPAA
b)  ERISA
*c) EMTALA
d)  Stark Law

Title: B06 Fee-for-service incentive
Points: 1
1.  In a fee-for-service payment model, the financial incentive most directly created for providers is to:
a)  Reduce the volume of services delivered per patient
*b) Increase the volume of billable services delivered
c)  Improve population-level health outcomes
d)  Lower per-patient total cost of care

Title: B07 Capitation definition
Points: 1
1.  Under capitation, a provider organization is paid:
a)  A separate fee for each service delivered to each patient
*b) A fixed amount per enrolled patient over a defined period, regardless of services used
c)  A bonus only when quality benchmarks are met
d)  A bundled payment for an episode of care

Title: B08 Bundled payment
Points: 1
1.  A bundled payment is best described as:
a)  A single fixed payment for all care a patient receives in a year
*b) A single payment covering all services associated with a defined episode of care
c)  A capitated payment shared across primary care providers
d)  A discount applied to fee-for-service rates after annual targets are met

Title: B09 Value-based purpose
Points: 1
1.  Value-based payment models are designed primarily to:
a)  Increase service volume
b)  Replace Medicaid with a uniform federal program
*c) Tie payment to measures of cost and quality rather than to volume alone
d)  Eliminate the role of private insurers

Title: B10 EPSDT
Points: 1
1.  The Medicaid Early and Periodic Screening, Diagnostic, and Treatment (EPSDT) benefit primarily serves which population?
a)  Pregnant adults
*b) Children and adolescents enrolled in Medicaid
c)  Adults aged 65 and older
d)  Dual-eligible beneficiaries

Title: B11 340B
Points: 1
1.  The 340B Drug Pricing Program requires drug manufacturers participating in Medicaid to provide outpatient drugs at discounted prices to:
a)  All retail pharmacies serving low-income patients
*b) Eligible safety-net providers and certain hospitals
c)  Medicare Part D plans
d)  Veterans Health Administration facilities

Title: B12 FQHC role
Points: 1
1.  Federally Qualified Health Centers (FQHCs) are best described as:
a)  Public hospitals operated by state health departments
*b) Community-based primary care providers that receive enhanced federal payment in exchange for serving medically underserved populations
c)  Specialty referral centers funded by the National Institutes of Health
d)  Private group practices that elect to follow Medicare rules

Title: B13 ERISA preemption
Points: 1
1.  ERISA preemption is most relevant to state efforts to regulate which type of insurance arrangement?
a)  State Medicaid managed care contracts
*b) Self-funded employer health plans
c)  Medicare Advantage plans
d)  Federally facilitated marketplace plans

Title: B14 Public health vs medical care
Points: 1
1.  Which of the following best distinguishes public health from medical care as institutional systems in the United States?
a)  Public health is funded only by states; medical care is funded only by the federal government.
*b) Public health primarily acts at the population level through surveillance, regulation, and prevention; medical care primarily acts at the individual level through diagnosis and treatment.
c)  Public health uses fee-for-service payment; medical care uses capitation.
d)  Public health serves only low-income populations; medical care serves everyone else.

Title: B15 Scope of practice
Points: 1
1.  State scope-of-practice laws most directly determine:
a)  How much Medicare pays for a given service
*b) Which clinical services a given category of clinician is legally permitted to provide and under what supervision
c)  Whether a hospital may participate in Medicaid
d)  Whether a drug may be sold in interstate commerce

Title: B16 Out-of-pocket cap Medicare
Points: 1
1.  Until recent reforms, traditional (Original) Medicare differed from most employer-sponsored plans in that it historically lacked:
a)  Any coverage of inpatient hospital care
*b) An annual out-of-pocket maximum for covered services
c)  Coverage of physician visits
d)  A premium structure

Title: B17 PPS hospital
Points: 1
1.  Medicare's inpatient prospective payment system (IPPS) pays hospitals primarily on the basis of:
a)  Itemized charges submitted by the hospital
*b) Diagnosis-related groups that classify each admission
c)  A fixed annual budget set by CMS
d)  A per-day rate negotiated with each hospital

Title: B18 Dual eligibles
Points: 1
1.  "Dual eligible" beneficiaries are individuals who are simultaneously enrolled in:
a)  Medicare and an employer plan
b)  Medicaid and CHIP
*c) Medicare and Medicaid
d)  TRICARE and the VA system

Title: B20 Medicaid administration
Points: 1
1.  Within federal rules, which level of government has the most direct authority over how Medicaid eligibility is determined and benefits are structured day-to-day?
a)  The federal Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services
*b) State Medicaid agencies
c)  County departments of public health
d)  Municipal hospital authorities


% =====================================================================
% SECTION 2 — SHORT-SCENARIO CAUSAL REASONING ITEMS
% These mirror the Exam 1 design philosophy: short text scenarios that
% test causal reasoning, feedback, delays, unintended consequences,
% endogenous dynamics. Many are multiple-select; partial logic loses points.
% These are the LLM-resistance core of the bank — answers depend on
% reading the scenario, not on retrieving definitions.
% =====================================================================

Title: C01 ED boarding
Points: 2
1.  A health system invests heavily in expanding emergency department (ED) capacity in a region where ED visits per capita have been climbing for a decade. Two years later, ED wait times are essentially unchanged, and ED visits per capita have continued to rise. Which explanation is most consistent with a systems-level account of this pattern?
a)  ED demand is fixed in the short run, so capacity expansions cannot affect wait times.
*b) Capacity expansion has reduced upstream barriers to ED use without altering the conditions producing demand, leaving the underlying drivers of ED utilization intact.
c)  Wait times are determined entirely by hospital staffing and are independent of patient volume.
d)  ED wait times always rise with capacity expansion because of regulatory delays in licensing new beds.

Title: C02 Insurance expansion and access
Points: 2
1.  Several years after a state expands Medicaid eligibility, surveys show that the share of low-income adults reporting they have a usual source of care has risen only modestly, despite a sharp drop in the uninsured rate. Which combination of system features best explains this pattern?
[*] Limited primary care clinician supply in the areas with the largest coverage gains
[*] Low Medicaid reimbursement that constrains provider willingness to accept new Medicaid patients
[ ] An overall national decline in the prevalence of chronic disease
[ ] A reduction in federal Medicaid matching rates following expansion
[*] Continued non-financial barriers such as transportation and clinic hours

Title: C03 Surveillance feedback
Points: 2
1.  A state public health department launches an aggressive new surveillance program for opioid-related overdoses, including improved data sharing with hospitals and coroners. Within 18 months, reported overdose deaths in the state rise sharply, even though independent indicators (EMS calls, naloxone use) are roughly flat. Which interpretation is most defensible?
a)  The new surveillance has caused an increase in actual overdose deaths.
*b) Improved surveillance has made existing overdose deaths more visible in official data; the underlying rate may not have changed substantially.
c)  Independent indicators are unreliable and should be discounted.
d)  Surveillance programs have no effect on reported counts of overdose deaths.

Title: C04 Insurance churn
Points: 2
1.  A Medicaid program tightens documentation requirements for annual eligibility renewal in order to reduce administrative error rates. Two years later, the share of beneficiaries with a gap in coverage of one or more months has roughly doubled, while the share enrolled in any given month has fallen. Which best describes what happened structurally?
a)  Tightening documentation reduced fraud without affecting access.
*b) The rule change shifted coverage loss from a flow problem (signing up) to a churn problem (cycling on and off), with predictable effects on continuity of care.
c)  Beneficiaries became less eligible because their incomes rose.
d)  The federal government reduced its Medicaid match rate.

Title: C05 Telehealth and parity
Points: 2
1.  A state passes a "telehealth payment parity" law requiring private insurers to pay the same rate for a telehealth visit as for an in-person visit. A health policy analyst predicts that this will improve access in rural areas. From a systems perspective, which of the following are reasonable concerns about whether parity alone will produce that outcome?
[*] Rural broadband infrastructure may be insufficient to support reliable video visits.
[*] Parity does not change the underlying supply of clinicians willing to serve rural patients.
[*] Parity may shift visits from in-person to telehealth without adding new capacity, leaving total visits roughly unchanged.
[ ] Parity automatically reduces hospital bed capacity in rural areas.
[ ] Parity is preempted by ERISA and cannot affect any private insurance plan.

Title: C06 Performative vs structural
Points: 2
1.  In response to a heat wave that produced a spike in heat-related ED visits and deaths, a city issues press releases urging residents to "check on neighbors" and "stay hydrated," and opens cooling centers during business hours on weekdays. From a systems perspective, this response is best characterized as:
a)  A structural change to the underlying drivers of heat vulnerability
*b) A primarily performative response that signals concern without altering housing quality, energy affordability, or labor protections that drive heat exposure
c)  A policy that addresses occupational stratification by race and gender
d)  A change in the rules governing Medicaid coverage of heat-related illness

Title: C07 Workforce and reimbursement
Points: 2
1.  A state has a long-standing shortage of primary care clinicians in rural counties. The legislature responds by authorizing 50 new primary care residency slots at the state's academic medical center, located in the largest urban area. Five years later, rural primary care clinician supply is essentially unchanged. Which of the following best explain this outcome from a systems perspective?
[*] Residency location strongly predicts eventual practice location, and the new slots are urban.
[*] Reimbursement and practice support in rural settings remain unattractive relative to urban or specialty alternatives.
[ ] Increasing residency slots has been shown to reduce overall clinician supply.
[*] The intervention targeted training capacity rather than the conditions that determine where trained clinicians choose to practice.
[ ] Rural patients no longer use primary care services.

Title: C08 Drug pricing leverage
Points: 2
1.  A policy proposal would allow Medicare to negotiate prices on a small set of high-cost drugs. Critics argue this will reduce pharmaceutical innovation. Supporters argue it will lower drug spending without major effects on innovation. From a systems perspective, which is most accurate about what this proposal does and does not change?
a)  It changes both the rules of payment and the underlying market concentration of the pharmaceutical industry.
*b) It changes one set of payment rules for one payer for a narrow set of drugs; it does not directly change patent rules, market concentration, or the broader incentives that shape drug pricing in the U.S.
c)  It is functionally identical to repealing patent protection for the negotiated drugs.
d)  It eliminates Medicare Part D.

Title: C09 Delay and policy frustration
Points: 2
1.  A state invests in expanded prenatal care services in counties with high rates of severe maternal morbidity. Three years later, severe maternal morbidity rates in those counties are essentially unchanged, and the legislature considers cutting the program. From a systems perspective, the strongest argument *against* immediate cuts is that:
a)  Programs once funded should not be cut for political reasons.
*b) The relevant outcome is shaped by long-acting structural conditions and accumulated risk; even effective interventions may take years to register in mortality and morbidity indicators, and short-term flatness is a weak basis for inference.
c)  Severe maternal morbidity is impossible to measure reliably at the county level.
d)  Cutting the program would violate ERISA preemption.

Title: C10 Sanctions and enforcement gap
Points: 2
1.  A state agency is responsible for enforcing nursing home staffing minimums, but its administrative staffing has been reduced by 40% over five years and inspections occur on average once every 22 months. The legislature responds to a high-profile abuse case by raising statutory penalties for violations. From a systems perspective, the most likely effect of this response is:
a)  A large reduction in violations because penalties are now higher.
*b) Little change in observed compliance, because enforcement capacity — not the size of the penalty — is the binding constraint.
c)  An immediate increase in nursing home closures.
d)  A reduction in the number of dual-eligible beneficiaries.

Title: C11 Risk selection
Points: 2
1.  A Medicare Advantage plan reports unusually low costs per enrollee compared to traditional Medicare. Which of the following are plausible system-level explanations that should be ruled out before concluding the plan is more efficient?
[*] Favorable risk selection — the plan disproportionately enrolls healthier beneficiaries.
[*] Coding intensity differences that increase risk-adjusted payment without changing care.
[ ] The plan does not actually pay providers.
[*] Network design that limits access to high-cost specialists or facilities.
[ ] The plan is exempt from federal regulation.

Title: C12 Endogenous data
Points: 2
1.  A county increases funding for child welfare investigations and adds investigative staff. Within two years, reported cases of child neglect rise sharply. A news headline reports that "neglect is surging." From a systems perspective, the most defensible interpretation is:
a)  Underlying neglect has clearly increased.
*b) The data are endogenous to the surveillance and enforcement system; increases in reported cases may reflect increased capacity to detect and record cases as much as, or more than, changes in underlying conditions.
c)  Investigative staffing has no effect on reported counts.
d)  The data prove that funding child welfare causes child neglect.

Title: C13 Safety-net displacement
Points: 2
1.  An academic medical center opens a new outpatient clinic in a low-income neighborhood previously served primarily by a Federally Qualified Health Center (FQHC). One year later, the FQHC reports a 25% drop in commercially insured patients and a worsening payer mix. Which of the following best describes what is happening at the system level?
a)  The FQHC has become less effective at delivering primary care.
*b) The new clinic is selectively drawing better-insured patients away from the FQHC, weakening the FQHC's cross-subsidization of uninsured and Medicaid patients.
c)  The FQHC has lost its 340B eligibility.
d)  Medicaid enrollment has fallen.

Title: C14 Coverage vs access
Points: 2
1.  A national policy reduces the uninsured rate from 16% to 9%. Two years later, evidence suggests that disparities in preventable hospitalizations between Black and white patients have not narrowed. Which of the following are consistent with a systems-level account of this finding?
[*] Insurance coverage addresses one barrier among several; structural inequities shaping exposure, access, and clinical encounter quality remain.
[*] Coverage gains may have been uneven across groups, with persistent gaps for the most affected populations.
[ ] Insurance coverage has no effect on health.
[*] Disparities are produced by reinforcing loops involving residential segregation, environmental exposure, and differential clinical treatment that a coverage policy does not directly disrupt.
[ ] Disparities are entirely explained by differences in genetic risk.

Title: C15 Capacity vs throughput
Points: 2
1.  A safety-net hospital adds 40 inpatient beds to address persistent ED boarding. Six months later, ED boarding times are unchanged. Which of the following is the most likely systems-level explanation?
a)  Adding beds has no relationship to boarding.
*b) Boarding is driven by throughput constraints (discharge planning, post-acute capacity, staffing) rather than by raw bed count, so adding beds without changing throughput leaves boarding unchanged.
c)  The hospital should close the new beds.
d)  EMTALA prohibits boarding.

Title: C16 Information flow leverage
Points: 2
1.  A health system's clinicians repeatedly order duplicate imaging studies because results from outside facilities are not visible in the electronic record. Two interventions are proposed: (A) a new institutional policy reminding clinicians to ask patients about prior imaging, and (B) integration of the regional health information exchange so outside images appear automatically. From a leverage-points perspective, which is the stronger structural intervention and why?
a)  A, because policies are easier to enforce than technology
*b) B, because it changes the information flow that is the proximate driver of duplicate ordering, rather than relying on individual clinician memory under time pressure
c)  A, because reminders always work
d)  Neither — duplicate imaging is unrelated to information flow.

Title: C17 Pricing and concentration
Points: 2
1.  Across many U.S. metropolitan areas, hospital prices for commercially insured patients have risen substantially over the past decade, while utilization has been roughly flat. Which set of factors is most consistent with a systems explanation of this pattern?
[*] Increased hospital and health-system market concentration following mergers
[*] Limited price transparency and negotiated rates that vary widely
[ ] Increased federal price regulation of commercial insurance
[*] Insurer market concentration that varies regionally and shapes negotiation leverage
[ ] A national decline in hospital staffing

Title: C18 Vaccination access vs trust
Points: 2
1.  A county with low childhood vaccination rates opens additional free vaccination clinics in underserved neighborhoods. Uptake rises modestly but remains well below state averages. Which of the following are most consistent with a systems account of why access expansion alone has limited effects on uptake?
[*] Trust in public health institutions varies across communities and is shaped by historical and ongoing experience.
[*] Information environments and social networks influence parental decisions in ways that clinic siting does not address.
[ ] Vaccines are only effective when administered in private clinics.
[*] Access is one element among several; provider recommendation, work and childcare schedules, and continuity of care also shape uptake.
[ ] Free vaccines are less effective than paid vaccines.

Title: C19 Cost-shifting myth
Points: 2
1.  A hospital administrator argues that the hospital must charge commercially insured patients more because Medicaid pays "below cost." Empirical research generally finds that the relationship between public payer rates and commercial prices is weaker than this story suggests. Which of the following best explains, in systems terms, why the simple cost-shifting story is incomplete?
a)  Hospitals do not actually treat Medicaid patients.
*b) Commercial prices are largely determined by negotiation leverage shaped by market concentration and contract structure, not by a mechanical pass-through of public payer shortfalls.
c)  Medicaid pays the same rates as commercial insurers.
d)  Hospitals are not permitted to set their own prices.

Title: C20 Medicaid unwinding
Points: 2
1.  After a federal continuous-enrollment requirement ends, a state begins redetermining Medicaid eligibility for all enrollees. Within a year, a substantial share of disenrollments are for procedural reasons (missing paperwork) rather than for income changes. From a systems perspective, this pattern most clearly reflects:
a)  Beneficiaries no longer being eligible due to rising incomes
*b) An interaction between administrative processing capacity, notification systems, and beneficiary information environments — a structural gap between governance and enforcement
c)  A reduction in the federal Medicaid matching rate
d)  An increase in fraud

Title: C21 Specialty referral bottleneck
Points: 2
1.  In a region with adequate primary care supply, patients referred to certain specialties (e.g., dermatology, endocrinology) wait 4–6 months for appointments. Which of the following are plausible structural drivers of this bottleneck?
[*] Specialist clinician supply is constrained relative to demand in the region.
[*] Reimbursement structures favor procedural over cognitive specialty care, shifting workforce composition.
[ ] Primary care clinicians are not allowed to make specialty referrals.
[*] Specialty practices selectively accept higher-paying insurance, narrowing effective access for Medicaid patients.
[ ] Specialty care is not covered by any insurance.

Title: C22 Behavioral health integration
Points: 2
1.  A primary care practice integrates a behavioral health clinician into its team to address unmet mental health need. Six months later, depression screening and follow-up rates have improved substantially. From a systems perspective, the most defensible interpretation is:
a)  Integration eliminates all barriers to mental health care.
*b) Integration reduced one set of access frictions (referral, stigma, scheduling) within a defined population; broader access barriers in the surrounding system remain.
c)  Mental health is now fully addressed in this region.
d)  Integration has no effect on screening rates.

Title: C23 Housing as health intervention
Points: 2
1.  A Medicaid managed care organization launches a "housing as health" pilot that provides supportive housing for high-cost Medicaid enrollees with histories of homelessness. After 18 months, hospital and ED utilization for the enrolled group falls. A state legislator proposes scaling the program statewide as a cost-saving measure. From a systems perspective, the most cautious and defensible reading is:
a)  Statewide scaling will produce identical savings.
*b) The pilot results may not generalize uniformly because effects depend on local housing market conditions, supportive service capacity, and the characteristics of the enrolled population.
c)  Housing has no relationship to health care utilization.
d)  The pilot is preempted by ERISA.

Title: C24 Reimbursement and prevention
Points: 2
1.  Despite repeated calls to "shift to prevention," the share of U.S. health spending on clinical preventive services has remained low for decades. Which of the following are systems-level explanations for this persistence?
[*] Fee-for-service payment structures reward volume of treatment over investment in prevention.
[*] Prevention benefits often accrue over long time horizons and to payers other than the one funding the intervention.
[ ] Prevention is illegal under federal law.
[*] Public health agencies — which lead much population-level prevention — are funded through separate, often discretionary streams that compete with other priorities.
[ ] There is no scientific basis for clinical prevention.

Title: C25 Rural hospital closure
Points: 2
1.  A rural hospital closes after several years of declining margins. Local commentators blame "Medicaid for paying too little." Which of the following are also plausible structural drivers, from a systems perspective?
[*] Population decline reducing the patient base
[*] Unfavorable payer mix concentrated in low-reimbursement payers
[*] Difficulty recruiting and retaining clinicians
[ ] Federal law requiring rural hospitals to close after 25 years
[*] Limited specialty referral and transfer infrastructure that constrains scope of services

Title: C26 Workforce pipeline delay
Points: 2
1.  A state projects a shortage of primary care clinicians and funds expanded medical and nursing education. From a systems perspective, the most accurate prediction about the timing of effects is:
a)  Clinician supply will rise within 6 months.
*b) Effects on practicing clinician supply will appear with a delay of years, because of training duration and the time required for graduates to enter and remain in practice.
c)  Funding education has no effect on clinician supply.
d)  The shortage will be resolved before any new students graduate.

Title: C27 Coverage as floor not ceiling
Points: 2
1.  A health policy student argues that "if everyone had insurance, health disparities would disappear." Which set of considerations most directly complicates this claim from a systems perspective?
[*] Insurance is one element shaping access; non-financial barriers and clinical encounter quality also vary across groups.
[*] Structural inequities in housing, environmental exposure, and economic security operate independently of insurance status.
[ ] Insurance has been shown to worsen health outcomes overall.
[*] Coverage products differ substantially in network breadth, cost-sharing, and administrative friction, producing unequal effective access even when nominal coverage is universal.
[ ] Insurance is irrelevant to health outcomes.

Title: C28 Information asymmetry
Points: 2
1.  Even when "price transparency" tools are made available to patients, evidence suggests that consumer "shopping" for elective procedures is rare. Which of the following best explains, in systems terms, why transparency alone has had limited effects on prices?
a)  Patients cannot read prices.
*b) Health care decisions involve clinical complexity, urgency, network constraints, and reliance on referrals — features that limit the conditions under which consumer shopping can discipline prices.
c)  Prices are fully regulated by the federal government.
d)  Transparency tools always show inaccurate prices.

Title: C29 Carve-outs and fragmentation
Points: 2
1.  A state Medicaid program "carves out" behavioral health services from its medical managed care contracts and contracts separately for behavioral health. Several years later, primary care providers report increased difficulty coordinating care for patients with both medical and behavioral health needs. Which best explains this outcome in systems terms?
a)  Carve-outs always improve coordination.
*b) Administrative separation of payment and reporting structures introduces friction in information flow and accountability across what is, clinically, a single patient population.
c)  Behavioral health is unrelated to primary care.
d)  Carve-outs increase the number of clinicians.

Title: C30 Workforce burnout loop
Points: 2
1.  A regional health system faces rising clinician turnover. Leadership responds with one-time retention bonuses. A year later, turnover has declined briefly and is rising again. Which of the following are systems-level reasons one-time bonuses tend to have limited durable effects?
[*] Bonuses do not change underlying workload, documentation burden, or staffing ratios.
[*] Burnout is sustained by reinforcing loops involving workload, moral injury, and reduced colleague support that bonuses do not address.
[ ] Bonuses are illegal under federal law.
[*] Departures of experienced clinicians increase load on remaining clinicians, accelerating further departures.
[ ] Clinician turnover is unrelated to working conditions.


