% SECTION 3 — MISCLASSIFICATION AND BOUNDARY ITEMS
% Endogenous vs. exogenous, structural vs. performative,
% leverage vs. symptom, governance vs. enforcement.
% Multiple-select; designed to punish surface pattern-matching.
% =====================================================================

Title: M01 Endogenous vs exogenous
Points: 2
1.  A team is analyzing the U.S. opioid epidemic and draws a system boundary that includes prescribing practices, drug supply dynamics, treatment access, and economic conditions in affected communities. Which of the following are reasonable to treat as ENDOGENOUS to this system as drawn? (Select all that apply.)
[*] Prescribing practices shaped by professional norms and pharmaceutical marketing within the system
[*] Treatment access shaped by Medicaid coverage rules and workforce supply within the system
[ ] A sudden global pandemic
[*] Economic conditions in affected communities, where the system explicitly includes them
[ ] Supply disruption due to global conflict

Title: M02 Performative vs structural
Points: 2
1.  Which of the following interventions are best characterized as PRIMARILY PERFORMATIVE rather than structural, in the sense used in this course? (Select all that apply.)
[*] A heat wave press release urging residents to "check on neighbors"
[ ] A change in Medicaid eligibility rules expanding adult coverage
[*] A hospital "wellness committee" that meets quarterly with no authority to change staffing
[*] An employer "mental health awareness week" with no change to benefits or workload
[ ] A statewide policy raising the minimum nurse-to-patient ratio

Title: M03 Leverage vs symptom
Points: 2
1.  A team identifies four candidate intervention points for reducing preventable maternal morbidity in their system map. Which two are most plausibly STRUCTURAL leverage points rather than symptomatic responses? (Select two.)
[*] Changing payment so that prenatal, intrapartum, and postpartum care are covered as an integrated bundle with quality accountability
[ ] Distributing pamphlets about warning signs in pregnancy at the hospital exit
[*] Changing Medicaid postpartum coverage from 60 days to 12 months
[ ] Adding a wall poster about hypertension to clinic waiting rooms

Title: M04 Governance vs enforcement
Points: 2
1.  Which of the following pairings correctly distinguishes a GOVERNANCE failure from an ENFORCEMENT failure?
a)  Governance: a state lacks inspectors. Enforcement: a state has no nursing home regulations.
*b) Governance: a state has not written rules requiring minimum staffing. Enforcement: a state has the rules but inspects nursing homes only every two years and rarely imposes sanctions.
c)  Governance and enforcement are the same thing.
d)  Governance and enforcement are both about clinical quality measures.

Title: M05 Stocks vs flows
Points: 2
1.  Which of the following are best understood as elements rather than relationships, in the sense used in this course? (Select all that apply.)
[*] Primary care clinician supply
[*] Uninsured population size
[ ] Annual Medicaid enrollment additions
[*] Hospital bed capacity
[ ] Monthly outpatient visits

Title: M06 Boundary discipline
Points: 2
1.  A team analyzing emergency department overcrowding draws a system boundary that excludes housing, behavioral health, and post-acute care capacity. Which is the strongest critique of this boundary choice?
a)  Boundaries should always be as wide as possible.
*b) The excluded factors are well-known structural drivers of ED demand and inpatient throughput, so excluding them is likely to obscure the dynamics the team is trying to explain.
c)  Boundaries do not affect analysis.
d)  Boundaries are determined by federal law.

Title: M07 Endogenous data
Points: 2
1.  Which of the following statements about data and systems is most consistent with the idea that DATA ARE OFTEN ENDOGENOUS to the systems that produce them?
a)  Data always describe an external reality independent of the system being measured.
*b) Many systems generate the data used to govern them, so apparent changes in measured outcomes can reflect changes in surveillance, classification, or reporting rather than changes in underlying conditions.
c)  Data are produced only by federal agencies.
d)  Data are unrelated to system structure.

Title: M08 Power and structure
Points: 2
1.  Which statement is most consistent with the course's treatment of power and structure?
a)  Systems thinking abstracts away from power; persistent harms are unintended.
*b) Rules, incentives, resource distributions, and institutional arrangements are core elements of system structure; persistent inequities are often the predictable result of reinforcing loops tied to political and economic incentives.
c)  Systems can only be analyzed if a single bad actor is identified.
d)  Systems thinking treats market arrangements as natural facts that cannot be changed.

Title: M09 Delay
Points: 2
1.  Which of the following scenarios most clearly illustrate the role of DELAY in system behavior? (Select all that apply.)
[*] A workforce education investment that produces no near-term change in clinician supply but a noticeable change after several years
[*] A heat-mitigation housing retrofit program whose effects on heat-related illness emerge only after multiple summers
[ ] A press release published the same day a policy is announced
[*] An expansion of preventive services whose effects on chronic disease morbidity appear only over years
[ ] A patient receiving same-day antibiotics for a confirmed bacterial infection

Title: M10 Symptom vs structure
Points: 2
1.  A health policy proposal frames the high U.S. uninsured rate among low-wage workers primarily as a problem of "individuals not signing up for available coverage." From a systems perspective, this framing is most accurately characterized as:
a)  A structural diagnosis that identifies upstream drivers
*b) A framing that locates a structurally produced outcome in individual behavior, drawing attention away from employer benefit design, eligibility complexity, premium affordability, and administrative friction
c)  An accurate causal account
d)  A description of a balancing feedback loop


